<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ophellas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 308 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ophellas was a Macedonian officer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> who conquered Cyrene for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I</a> in 322 BCE and governed it. He later made himself independent, and in 308 BCE he led an army west to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agathocles-of-syracuse">Agathocles of Syracuse</a> in his war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. Agathocles had him killed and took over his troops.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ophellas.jpg" alt="Ophellas" width="320" height="157" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ophellas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ophellas, also spelt Ophelas, was born in Pella, the son of a man named Seilenus. He is first mentioned in 326 BCE as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trierarchy">trierarch</a>, the commander of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">trireme</a>, in Alexander's fleet on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>; nothing else is known of his earlier service under Alexander. After Alexander's death he followed the fortunes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I Soter</a> and went with him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 322 BCE Ptolemy sent him at the head of a considerable army to take advantage of a civil war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a>. Ophellas defeated the Cyrenaeans who had sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thibron">Thibron</a>, captured Thibron himself and handed him over to the people of Teucheira, who tortured and executed him. Ptolemaic control over Cyrene and its dependencies was established, with Ophellas as governor. A long constitutional document from Cyrene, preserved on stone, sheds light on the new pro-Ptolemaic regime, though its precise date is disputed.</p><p>His later career is poorly attested. Justin calls him king of Cyrene and the Suda its dynast. He married Eurydice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miltiades">Miltiades</a>, and had a son by her, also named Miltiades; he seems to have kept friendly relations with Athens. His name does not appear in the accounts of the Cyrenaean revolt of 313 BCE, which Ptolemy quickly suppressed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 309/8 BCE Agathocles of Syracuse, then at war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, proposed an alliance: Ophellas would keep whatever their combined forces conquered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and Agathocles would reserve only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> for himself. Ophellas raised a powerful army, partly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, where many citizens resented the loss of their voting rights, and reached Carthaginian territory after a perilous march of more than two months. He was received with every show of friendship and even adopted Agathocles as a son.</p><p>The alliance was short-lived. In November 308 BCE, while many of Ophellas's soldiers were away foraging, Agathocles accused him of plotting and attacked his camp, and Ophellas died fighting. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polyaenus">Polyaenus</a> adds that Agathocles had earlier sent his son Heracleides to keep him off his guard. The leaderless army was absorbed into the forces of Agathocles, and Ptolemy afterwards took direct control of Cyrene.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ophellas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 324 to 322 BCE the Spartan adventurer Thibron attacked the city, and its factions called in Ptolemy I, whose general <b>Ophellas</b> annexed Cyrene to the Ptolemaic Empire in 322 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-cyrene">Republic of Cyrene</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pella, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.76,22.53&amp;year=-318" aria-label="Pella on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5102 708 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ophellas"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5552 978h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5552 978h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.76,22.53&amp;year=-318">What was here in 318 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
